    "text": "Key Differences Aspect Basic Timestamp Ordering Strict Timestamp Ordering Operation Operations are executed immediately if they Write operations wait until earlier Execution obey timestamp rules. transactions commit. Cascading May occur (due to immediate operations). Avoided because writes wait for earlier Rollbacks transactions. Complexity Simpler but less restrictive. More restrictive to ensure data consistency. Example in a Timestamp Queue Scenario: Two transactions T1 (TS = 1) and T2 (TS = 2) are working on a bank account balance (initial = %500). Basic Timestamp Ordering: 1. T1 (TS = 1) reads the balance (2500) and writes a new balance (2400). - RTS(A) = 1, WTS(A) = 1. 2. T2 (TS = 2) tries to write %300. - Allowed because TS(T2) > WTS(A). 3. T2 writes 3300, updating WTS(A) = 2. Result: Final balance = %300. Strict Timestamp Ordering: 1. T1 (TS = 1) reads the balance (2500) and writes a new balance (2400). + T1 must commit before any operation from T2 can proceed. 2. T2 (TS = 2) waits until Ti commits before writing %300. Result: Final balance = 3300, but changes are only applied after T1 commits.",
